          %blockquote
            %p
              This page is about formalizing the notion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+sequences"} spectral sequences
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2013/08/what_is_a_spectral_sequence.html"} n-category cafe
              and the other on the
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org/2013/08/08/spectral-sequences/"} HoTT blog
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#References"} below
          %h1#spectral_sequences_in_homotopy_type_theory Spectral sequences in homotopy type theory
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#what_is_a_spectral_sequence"} What is a spectral sequence?
              %li
                %a{:href => "#spectral_sequences_in_hott"} Spectral Sequences in HoTT
              %li
                %a{:href => "#on_indexing_choices"} On indexing choices
              %li
                %a{:href => "#notes"} Notes
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
          %h2#what_is_a_spectral_sequence What is a spectral sequence?
          %p
            There are many answers to “what is a
            = succeed "?\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+sequence"} spectral sequence
          %blockquote
            %p Spectral sequences are to long exact sequences as iterated extensions are to extensions.
          %p (Without knowing anything else, the terminology suggests that spectral sequences could also be called “iterated long exact sequences”.)
          %p
            There are four phrases here that I need to explain, and two more concepts that have a ghostly presence (fibration sequences and iterated fibration sequences). First of all, an
            %em extension
            is a sequence of morphisms of abelian groups (for simplicity — more generally we could be in any abelian category):
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi B
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi g
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \xrightarrow{f} B \xrightarrow{g} C
          %p
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is the inclusion of the kernel of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is the quotient of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            by the image of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is injective,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is surjective, and the image of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is equal to the kernel of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi id
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi proj
                        %mn 2
                  %mi C
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \xrightarrow{(id,0)} A\oplus C \xrightarrow{proj_2} C.
          %p
            An extension that’s isomorphic to one of this form is said to be
            = succeed "." do
              %em split
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            as “put together” in some more general way from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is an “extension of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            by
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is always isomorphic to the cartesian product
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            as a sort of “twisted product” of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ext
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ext^1(C,A)
          %p
            Thinking of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            as put together from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=C=0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B=0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B=0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=C=0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B=C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=0
            we have
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is finite, or finitely generated, if and only if both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            are.
          %p
            Next, a
            %em long exact sequence
            (LES) is a longer sequence of morphisms of abelian groups
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \xrightarrow{f_{n+1}} A_{n+1} \xrightarrow{f_n} A_n \xrightarrow{f_{n-1}} \cdots
          %p
            such that for each
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n
            is equal to the kernel of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{n-1}
            (called “exactness at
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi B
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi g
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to A \xrightarrow{f} B \xrightarrow{g} C \to 0
          %p
            (a “short exact sequence”), since in such a sequence, exactness at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            just means injectivity of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            just means surjectivity of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %em fibration sequence
            is a sequence of maps of pointed spaces
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi Y
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi g
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \xrightarrow{f} Y \xrightarrow{g} Z
          %p
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is (up to homotopy) the inclusion of the homotopy fiber of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            over the basepoint. (By “space” I really mean “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-groupoid”, which a classical topologist could read as “topological space up to weak homotopy equivalence” or “simplicial set up to weak homotopy equivalence”, and a homotopy type theorist could read as “type”.) The simplest example is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Z
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to X\times Z \to Z,
          %p
            the “trivial bundle” over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            with fiber
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            as being “put together” from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            in some more general way, as a “twisted product”. Note that this is very similar to how we think of an extension, except that we’ve replaced our abelian groups with spaces.
          %p
            Of course, a space is not exactly an abelian group, but it contains many abelian groups, namely its homotopy groups. So we might expect that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is a twisted product of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            as above, that its homotopy groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(Y)
            would be twisted products of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(Z)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X) \to \pi_n(Y) \to \pi_n(Z)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            can “interact” in a nontrivial way; but instead we have a
            %em long
            exact sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \to \pi_{n+1}(X) \to \pi_{n+1}(Y) \to \pi_{n+1}(Z) \to \pi_n(X) \to \pi_n(Y) \to \pi_n(Z) \to \cdots.
          %p
            This LES can be regarded as expressing a way in which the groups
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(Y)
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X)
            = succeed "\u2019s." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(Z)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X)=0
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(Y) = \pi_n(Z)
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(Y)=0
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X) = 0 = \pi_n(Z)
            we get
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X) = \pi_{n+1}(Z)
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            (and indeed, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is contractible, then
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \Omega Z
          %p
            Another way to express the LES of a fibration, which looks more like what we’ll see for spectral sequences later on, is that there exist maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_n : \pi_n(Z) \to \pi_{n-1}(X)
            and extensions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi im
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ker
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(\delta_{n+1}) \to \pi_n(Y) \to ker(\delta_n).
          %p
            In other words, instead of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(Y)
            being put together from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(Z)
            %em subgroup
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(Z)
            and a
            %em quotient
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            there’s a functor from the category of fibration sequences to the category of extensions, sending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y\to Z
            to the above extension.
          %p
            Of course,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n
            is only an abelian group for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\ge 2
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=0
            = succeed "," do
              %em spectra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n
            is an abelian group not only for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\ge 0
            but for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\in \mathbb{Z}
          %p
            Now let’s move on to the other side of the analogy, starting with iterated extensions. By an
            %em iterated extension
            I mean a sequence of extensions of the following form:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mi s
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mi s
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mi s
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo −
                              %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\vdots \\
                  A_{s+1} \to B_{s+1} \to B_s\\
                  A_s \to B_{s} \to B_{s-1} \\
                  A_{s-1} \to B_{s-1} \to B_{s-2}\\
                  \\vdots
                  }
          %p
            The intent here is to regard
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mo ≡
                  %mi lim
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi s
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B :\equiv \lim (\cdots \to B_{s+1} \to B_s \to \cdots)
            as “put together” from the
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_s
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo :
                    %mo ≡
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} colim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi s
                    %mo →
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C :\equiv \colim (\cdots \to B_{s+1} \to B_s \to \cdots)
            = succeed "\u2019s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_s
            = succeed "\u2019s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_s
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo →
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s\to\infty
            implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is the eventual value, while eventual constancy as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s\to-\infty
            implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is that eventual value. In the latter case it simplifies things with no essential loss of generality to end with
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 0
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 0
                          %mo →
                          %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                          %mo →
                          %mn 0
                          %mo →
                          %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\vdots \\
                  A_{1} \to B_{1} \to B_0\\
                  B_0 \to B_{0} \to 0 \\
                  0 \to 0 \to 0 \\
                  \\vdots
                  }
          %p
            so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=0
            and the
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_s
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            (with
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0 = B_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B=0
            if and only if all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi s
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_s=0
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi lim
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim^1
          %p
            It’s more common to describe iterated extensions in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %em filtrations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi s
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \subseteq F_s B \subseteq F_{s-1} B \subseteq \cdots \subseteq B
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi s
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_s B
            is the kernel of the projection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to B_s
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi s
                    %mo =
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi s
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_s = B/F_s B
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi s
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi s
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_s = F_{s-1} B/F_s B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mi s
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mi B
                          %mo →
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mi B
                          %mo →
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mi s
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mi s
                          %mi B
                          %mo →
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mi s
                          %mi B
                          %mo →
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\vdots\\
                  F_s B / F_{s+1} B \to B / F_{s+1}B \to B/F_s B\\
                  F_{s-1} B / F_{s} B \to B / F_{s}B \to B/F_{s-1} B\\
                  \\vdots
                  }
          %p
            One problem with this point of view is that just having a filtration doesn’t ensure that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is the limit
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi lim
                      %mi s
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi s
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim_s B/ F_s B
            = succeed "." do
              %em complete and Hausdorff
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/uniform+space"} uniformity
            induced on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            by the filtration.) The other condition we might want is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mi s
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi s
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = B / \bigcup_s F_s B
            is zero, i.e. that
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mi s
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi s
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup_s F_s B = B
            = succeed "." do
              %em exhaustive
            %em finite
            iterated extensions, however, these conditions are all automatic.
          %p
            A more serious problem with the filtration point of view is that it relies on the fact that the quotient of the kernel of a surjection is that same surjection — otherwise we couldn’t recover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_s
            from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi s
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_s B
            %em have
            to consider instead an
            = succeed "," do
              %em iterated fibration sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mi s
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi s
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mi s
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo −
                              %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\vdots \\
                  X_{s+1} \to Y_{s+1} \to Y_s\\
                  X_s \to Y_s \to Y_{s-1}\\
                  X_{s-1} \to Y_{s-1} \to Y_{s-2}\\
                  \\vdots
                  }
          %p
            As before, we view this as expressing the limit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %mo ≡
                  %msub
                    %mi lim
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mi s
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y :\equiv \lim_s (\cdots \to Y_{s+1}\to Y_s \to \cdots)
            as put together out of the
            = succeed "\u2018s." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_s
            %em tower of fibrations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mi s
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \to Y_{s+1}\to Y_s \to Y_{s-1} \to \cdots
          %p
            since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_s
            is determined as the (homotopy) fiber of the map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mi s
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_s \to Y_{s-1}
          %p
            (If we were working with spectra instead of spaces, then from an iterated fibration sequence we could define a “filtration” of the limit
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi s
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_s Y
            is the fiber of the map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\to Y_s
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_s
            %em would
            be the cofiber of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi s
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_s Y \to Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi s
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_s Y \to Y
            would not necessarily be “injective” in any sense.)
          %p
            The goal now is to extract some algebraic relationship among the groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X_s)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(Y)
            in an iterated fibration sequence, which relate to iterated extensions just as LES’s relate to extensions. Of course, the first thing we get is a sequence of LES’s:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mi s
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo →
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi s
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mi s
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi s
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo →
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\vdots\\
                  \\cdots \to \pi_n(X_{s+1}) \to \pi_n(Y_{s+1}) \to \pi_n(Y_s) \to \pi_{n-1}(X_{s+1}) \to \cdots \\
                  \\cdots \to \pi_n(X_{s}) \to \pi_n(Y_{s}) \to \pi_n(Y_{s-1}) \to \pi_{n-1}(X_{s}) \to \cdots \\
                  \\vdots
                  }
          %p
            These sequences are related in that each group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(Y_s)
            appears in
            %em two
            of them. This enables us to construct the following composite “connecting maps”
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X_s) \to \pi_n(Y_s) \to \pi_{n-1}(X_{s+1}).
          %p
            relating the homotopy groups of the fibers
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_s
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi d
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d^2
            (note that this does
            %em not
            mean the square of a map
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
