                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \xrightarrow{d^2} \pi_n(X_s) \xrightarrow{d^2} \pi_{n-1}(X_{s+1}) \xrightarrow{d^2} \pi_{n-2}(X_{s+2}) \xrightarrow{d^2} \cdots.
          %p Of course, if we’re experienced homological-algebraists, the first thing that we want to do with a chain complex is take its homology, but let’s step back a second to make sure that that’s a sensible thing to do.
          %p
            A
            %em cycle
            in this chain complex is an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in \pi_n(X_s)
            that becomes zero in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
