          %p
            Of course, the
            %em interesting
            applications of spectral sequences are less trivial, but this post is already quite long enough. In the companion post on the HoTT blog, I’ll talk about how to use this spectral sequence (with spectra) to extract versions of the Atiyah-Hirzebruch and Serre spectral sequences, and see what they can do for us.
          %p
            One final teaser: note that while the groups in the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^{r+1}
            page of a spectral sequence are determined by the groups and the differentials in the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^r
            page, the differential in the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^{r+1}
            page is not so determined. To figure out what it is, we need to know about the rest of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi r
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} th
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r^{\mathrm{th}}
            exact couple, which can be tedious to work out. In many applications it suffices to know that such a differential
            = succeed "," do
              %em exists
            %em constructive
            definition of the whole exact couple and spectral sequence, as we would obtain by implementing the definition in a hypothetical fully constructive version of homotopy type theory, then the definition would be a program, and we could simply ask the computer to evaluate it on any input we cared about. This suggests to me a vague possibility of a way that homotopy type theory might conceivably one day be useful even to the “working algebraic topologist”.
          %h2#spectral_sequences_in_hott Spectral Sequences in HoTT
          %p
            Now we want to construct the cohomological Serre spectral sequence of a fibration (i.e. a type family). First recall that a
            %strong spectrum
            is a family of pointed types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi Type
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y : \mathbb{N} \to \mathsf{Type}_\ast
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi Ω
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_n = \Omega Y_{n+1}
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %strong cohomology
            of a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with coefficients in a spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            to be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
